<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Xia dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">2070 BCE – 1600 BCE (approx.) · capital Yangcheng</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">China's traditional first dynasty, founded by the flood-taming Yu the Great; whether it was a real state remains one of the great open questions of Chinese archaeology.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Later histories, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian</a>, describe the Xia as the dynasty founded when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yu-the-great">Yu the Great</a>, rewarded for controlling the floods of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, passed power to his son Qi, making rule hereditary. The register follows the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xia-shang-zhou-chronology-project">Xia-Shang-Zhou Chronology Project</a>'s dates. No writing survives from the period itself.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Everything known comes from texts written a thousand years later or from archaeology. Many scholars link the Xia with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erlitou-culture">Erlitou culture</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> basin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, which built palace compounds on rammed-earth platforms, cast the earliest Chinese bronze ritual vessels and made jade blades. Whether Erlitou was the Xia, and what its people called themselves, cannot be proved without inscriptions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Tradition holds that the last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jie-of-xia">Jie</a>, was a tyrant overthrown by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang dynasty</a>, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mingtiao">Battle of Mingtiao</a>. The Xia became the model of the first legitimate dynasty, and later Chinese political thought used its fall as the classic example of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian">Heaven</a> withdrawing its favour from a corrupt ruler.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/xia-dynasty.jpg" alt="Xia dynasty" width="320" height="276"><figcaption>Image: w:Gurdjieff (Lamassu Design) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xia_dynasty.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2070 BCE – 1600 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/21st-century-bce">21st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century-bce">17th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yangcheng</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Xia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1835&amp;focus=xia-dynasty" aria-label="Xia dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7553 930 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Xia dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7873 1186L7880 1130"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7880 1130h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7873 1186h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7873 1186h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=-1835&amp;focus=xia-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
